      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x - 3 y + 3 z)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x - 3 y + 3 z)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x - 3 y + 3 z)\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(-3)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3)\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(-3)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3)\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(-3)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3)\\,\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((1)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t)+(3)\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =(2)+(- 6 t)+(6 t) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
